  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nToday and Tonight ...\n\nThe main story of this forecast package is the evolution of the\nupper-level trough that is currently situated through the\nsouthern Great Plains. Amidst this parent trough is an embedded\nshortwave, currently lifting to the northeast. This shortwave,\nand its surface low-pressur center in northwestern Missouri,\nsupporting the dreary conditions across much of the region, \nwith the main rain band just east of Highway 281. As this low \npressure center continues to move northeastward, this rain band \nwill also slide eastward over the next few hours, bringing an \nend to the rainfall area wide this evening (though some western \nlocations have already seen an end to this rain). Likewise, \nstrong northerly winds, gusting up to 40-45 MPH, will gradually \ndecrease through the afternoon and evening hours, with gusts \nabove 25 MPH eventually tapering off tomorrow morning. However, \nthe low stratus layer is expected to continue through the night \nfor most of the area (especially areas east of Highway 183). \nThis cloud cover, along with the cold air advection behind the \nfront, will limit the temperature fluctuation today, with highs \nin the upper 40s to mid 50s (a temperature fluctuation of up to \n5 degrees). Low temperatures tomorrow morning will primarily be \nin the 30s, with about a 30-60% chance of seeing temperatures \ndrop below freezing northwest of a line from roughly Bertrand to\nOrd (which will largely depend on how much clearing will be on \nthe back side of this system).\n\nTomorrow Into Thursday...\n\nAs the shortwave trough continues to move off northeast, the\ncloudy skies will start to clear the area by mid morning\ntomorrow. However, high temperatures tomorrow will still be\nlimited to the upper 40s to 50s, as breezy northwesterly winds \ngusting up to 25-30 MPH are possible, particularly north of \nHighway 30. As the main upper level low stalls over Minnesota\nand Iowa, another shortwave trough passes over the area\nWednesday night into Thursday morning. This upper-level\ndisturbance brings with it a 15-20% chance of rain showers\ntomorrow evening into early Thursday morning, though widespread\nprecipitation is not expected. Furthermore, cloudy skies are\nexpected to build back into the area tomorrow night through\nThursday afternoon, limiting highs on Thursday in the 50s. \n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nAfter Thursday, the rest of the forecast period is expected to\nbe mostly dry as an upper-level ridge starts to build over the\nplains, though there is a nonzero (less than 10%) chance of \nprecipitation Saturday night into Sunday. Temperatures are \nexpected to be near-normal, with highs increasing to the low \n60s Saturday through Monday.",
